What is the output of:
SELECT name FROM users WHERE id IN (SELECT user_id FROM orders WHERE amount > 1000);

If there are 5 users with no high-value orders?

What is the difference in performance between:
A: SELECT * FROM users WHERE id IN (SELECT user_id FROM orders);
B: SELECT DISTINCT u.* FROM users u JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id;

What is the result of:
SELECT name FROM users WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT user_id FROM orders WHERE user_id IS NOT NULL);

Assuming some orders have user_id = NULL?

What is the output of:
SELECT (SELECT name FROM users LIMIT 1) AS first_user;

If the users table has 1000 rows?

Performance: A large orders table is joined to itself via a subquery:
SELECT o1.* FROM orders o1 WHERE o1.id IN (SELECT MIN(id) FROM orders o2 WHERE o1.user_id = o2.user_id GROUP BY o2.user_id);

Why might this be slow?
